{
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Light behaves as a wave, so it can show **diffraction** as well as interference.\nDiffraction is the spreading of a wave past an edge or through an opening, into regions beyond its straight-ahead path."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Water waves also spread this way, so a wave picture of light cannot treat light only as a thin straight line.\nWavelength describes the distance over which a wave repeats in space."
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "Frequency describes how many oscillations occur at one position per unit of time.\nOne describes space and the other describes time, so a short wavelength does not mean fewer oscillations."
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "In the same medium, when the wave speed is the same, a higher frequency means a shorter wavelength.\nThe source explains that spreading is especially noticeable when the obstacle is comparable in size to the wavelength."
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 4,
      "text": "Visible light has a very short wavelength, while everyday obstacles are usually much larger.\nThat makes diffraction hard to notice directly, not absent."
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "A narrow beam passing through a small hole makes a spot on a screen beyond it.\nA drawing that allows only straight-line travel predicts a smaller spot."
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The actual spot spreads farther, showing that straight rays alone do not describe the wave after the opening.\nThis is the departure from straight-line propagation described in the source, not a failure of wave behavior.\nThe change concerns direction and the spread of the spot, not a change of frequency caused by passing through a stationary opening."
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Young's experiment.** According to the source, in 1802, after discovering light interference, Young carried out the classic diffraction experiment shown in Figure 203.\nHe used a pin to make two nearby holes, $B$ and $C$, in an opaque screen and illuminated them with a narrow beam through hole $A$ in another screen."
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The source stresses that illuminating both holes from the same beam was the crucial detail.\nBy Huygens' principle, the spherical wave spreading from hole $A$ produces coherent oscillations at holes $B$ and $C$."
    },
    {
      "index": 9,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "Here, coherent means having the same frequency and a phase difference that stays steady.\nPhase describes where an oscillation is within its repeating cycle."
    },
    {
      "index": 10,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "A steady phase difference allows a stable pattern of bright and dark fringes.\nIn this observation, the source statement that only coherent waves can interfere refers to producing stable, recognizable fringes."
    },
    {
      "index": 11,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 4,
      "text": "Incoherent waves can also overlap, but their changing phase relationship does not maintain that pattern.\nDiffraction spreads the light from holes $B$ and $C$ so the two beams can overlap on the observing screen."
    },
    {
      "index": 12,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 5,
      "text": "Where their oscillations reinforce one another, the screen is brighter.\nWhere their oscillations cancel one another, it is darker."
    },
    {
      "index": 13,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 6,
      "text": "The curved lines show sections of wavefronts, surfaces whose points are at the same phase at one instant, not many separate thin rays.\nDiffraction spreads the beams, while interference determines the brightness pattern where they overlap."
    },
    {
      "index": 14,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 7,
      "text": "Young found that covering one hole removed the two-hole interference fringes, although light from the remaining hole could still diffract.\nThe source credits this experiment with the first, extremely precise measurements of wavelengths for different colors of light."
    },
    {
      "index": 15,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Fresnel's theory.** The source presents Fresnel's work as an important advance in the study of diffraction.\nHe studied different diffraction effects in detail and developed a quantitative theory."
    },
    {
      "index": 16,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Quantitative means being able to calculate the resulting oscillation at different places, rather than only describing what might happen.\nThose calculations predict the bright and dark parts of a pattern."
    },
    {
      "index": 17,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The source says his theory made it possible in principle to calculate interference patterns caused by different obstacles.\nIt also credits him with the first wave-based explanation of why light in a uniform medium usually appears to travel in straight lines.\nDiffraction and the apparent straight path can therefore be understood within the same wave picture."
    },
    {
      "index": 18,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Fresnel combined Huygens' principle with the interference of secondary waves.\nHuygens' principle treats positions on a wavefront as sources of secondary waves."
    },
    {
      "index": 19,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Their oscillations must be combined at the point being studied to find the total effect there.\nThe source refers back to a brief account in its fourth chapter, a reference to the original book rather than a new chapter in this lesson."
    },
    {
      "index": 20,
      "blockIndex": 7,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "To calculate the light-wave amplitude at a point in space, imagine a closed surface surrounding the light source.\nA closed surface is like a shell with no opening, chosen for the calculation rather than placed there to block the light."
    },
    {
      "index": 21,
      "blockIndex": 7,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Treat positions on it as secondary sources and combine the waves they send to the point being studied.\nAmplitude describes the greatest departure of an oscillation from its equilibrium state."
    },
    {
      "index": 22,
      "blockIndex": 7,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "Phase tells us how the arriving oscillations line up in their cycles.\nWe must therefore consider reinforcement and cancellation, not simply add the sizes of all the amplitudes.\nThe combined result determines the light-wave amplitude at that point."
    },
    {
      "index": 23,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "The point source S sends out spherical waves, and we consider how light reaches the observation point $B$ in Figure 204.\nThe curved line is part of a spherical wavefront of radius $R$, not a round plate blocking light."
    },
    {
      "index": 24,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "When its secondary waves are combined at point $B$, the source describes an effective result approximately like light arriving from the small surface patch $ab$ at point $B$.\nIn this calculation, contributions from much of the rest of the spherical surface cancel one another."
    },
    {
      "index": 25,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The result is as if light traveled mainly along the straight line $SB$.\nAs if matters: it describes the combined effect, not a claim that waves elsewhere do not exist.\nThe small patch illustrates this approximation, and the diagram supplies no numerical value for its size."
    },
    {
      "index": 26,
      "blockIndex": 11,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Predicting patterns.** Fresnel also studied diffraction by different kinds of obstacles quantitatively.\nThe same wave theory needed to account for different pattern shapes, not just one effect."
    },
    {
      "index": 27,
      "blockIndex": 12,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "The source recounts that in 1818, a surprising result was discussed at a meeting of the French Academy of Sciences.\nAn unnamed scholar pointed out a prediction of Fresnel's theory."
    },
    {
      "index": 28,
      "blockIndex": 12,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "For the particular hole size and source-to-hole and hole-to-screen distances described by the source, the center of the spot behind a round hole should be dark.\nBehind a small opaque disk, the center of the shadow should instead contain a bright spot."
    },
    {
      "index": 29,
      "blockIndex": 12,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The source reports that experiments confirmed these effects, surprising the scholars.\nWaves spreading past edges can combine to make a center dark or bright, something a drawing of straight rays and shadow edges alone cannot explain."
    },
    {
      "index": 30,
      "blockIndex": 12,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "The center of a round-hole pattern depends on the hole size, distances, and other conditions;\nit is not always dark.\nThis passage supplies neither numerical sizes and distances nor the name of the scholar, so none are added here."
    },
    {
      "index": 31,
      "blockIndex": 13,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Patterns from different obstacles.** Light has a short wavelength, so spreading angles are usually small when an opening or obstacle is much larger than that wavelength.\nIn the experiments discussed by the source, placing the observing screen far from the obstacle makes diffraction easier to see."
    },
    {
      "index": 32,
      "blockIndex": 13,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "This is especially useful for the small bright and dark features just described.\nOver a longer distance, even a small angle of spreading can produce a noticeable separation on the screen.\nWavelength, obstacle or opening size, and observation distance must therefore be considered together."
    },
    {
      "index": 33,
      "blockIndex": 14,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Figure 205 shows photographs of diffraction patterns produced by three kinds of obstacles.\nThe letters below identify panels, not exercise questions."
    },
    {
      "index": 34,
      "blockIndex": 14,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "From left to right, their source identities are as follows.\na) A thin metal wire produces bright and dark bands running along the wire direction."
    },
    {
      "index": 35,
      "blockIndex": 14,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "b) A round opening produces bright and dark rings around the center.\nc) A round opaque object produces a bright dot in the center of its dark shadow, with surrounding ring-like changes in brightness."
    },
    {
      "index": 36,
      "blockIndex": 14,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "The photographs show different brightness distributions, but provide no scale for measuring distances.\nThe center of the round-opening example appears dark;\nthis is one set of conditions, not the appearance of every round-opening pattern."
    },
    {
      "index": 37,
      "blockIndex": 17,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Resolution is not magnification.** A microscope can make an image larger, but the wave nature of light limits how fine a structure it can distinguish.\nResolution means recognizing nearby details as separate parts, not merely making their image look big."
    },
    {
      "index": 38,
      "blockIndex": 17,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Diffraction spreads the image of a tiny object into a pattern with a finite width instead of an indefinitely sharp point.\nIf patterns from neighboring details merge, magnification makes the merged image larger without automatically separating the details."
    },
    {
      "index": 39,
      "blockIndex": 17,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The source uses objects smaller than the wavelength as an example of this difficulty in ordinary optical observation.\nThis describes the diffraction limit of an ordinary microscope, not an absolute size boundary for every imaging method under every condition."
    },
    {
      "index": 40,
      "blockIndex": 17,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "Resolution also depends on the range of angles over which light is collected, the imaging method, and the rule used to judge separation.\nMagnification alone does not settle whether details can be distinguished."
    },
    {
      "index": 41,
      "blockIndex": 18,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Diffraction also limits a telescope.\nIts objective is the light-collecting lens facing the observed object, and its opening has a boundary."
    },
    {
      "index": 42,
      "blockIndex": 18,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Diffraction at this finite opening makes a star image a central bright spot with fainter bright and dark rings, rather than an ideal point.\nIf two stars lie in very similar directions in the sky, their patterns overlap and can be hard to recognize as two light sources."
    },
    {
      "index": 43,
      "blockIndex": 18,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "Angular separation means the angle between the viewing directions, not the actual distance between the stars in space.\nThe source says the limiting angular separation is determined by the ratio of wavelength to objective diameter."
    },
    {
      "index": 44,
      "blockIndex": 18,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "A smaller ratio helps distinguish nearby directions when the observing method and separation criterion are the same.\nA larger objective diameter or a shorter wavelength therefore usually improves diffraction-limited resolution.\nThe source gives no exact factor or separation criterion here, so it does not support an exact numerical limit."
    },
    {
      "index": 45,
      "blockIndex": 19,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**When straight rays are useful.** Geometrical optics represents light by rays, which are useful for describing paths, shadows, reflection, and refraction.\nDiffraction shows that this is an approximate model, not a complete picture at every scale."
    },
    {
      "index": 46,
      "blockIndex": 19,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The source emphasizes obstacles much larger than the wavelength as the condition for applying straight-line propagation and the other laws of geometrical optics.\nRead together with its statement about approximation, this means the ray model is very accurate when diffraction is negligible for the scale being studied."
    },
    {
      "index": 47,
      "blockIndex": 19,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "It does not mean wave behavior disappears or that every experimental error is absent.\nWhen openings, details near edges, or structures to be resolved approach the wavelength scale, spreading and interference must be considered instead of relying only on straight rays."
    },
    {
      "index": 48,
      "blockIndex": 21,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**Augustin Fresnel.** The source identifies Augustin Fresnel, born in 1788 and deceased in 1827, as a French physicist.\nThe adjacent source portrait represents this historical physicist, not a fictional character."
    },
    {
      "index": 49,
      "blockIndex": 21,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The source credits Fresnel with laying the foundations of modern wave optics.\nHe supplemented Huygens' principle with interference between secondary waves and developed a quantitative theory of diffraction.\nThese contributions connected observed brightness patterns with a wave model that could be used for calculation."
    },
    {
      "index": 50,
      "blockIndex": 22,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "The source credits Fresnel with first establishing the **transverse nature of light waves**.\nTransverse means that the oscillation direction is perpendicular to the direction of travel."
    },
    {
      "index": 51,
      "blockIndex": 22,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "It is not another name for the spreading of a wave past an edge.\nThe earlier wavefront drawings help describe propagation and overlap, but their curved lines alone do not establish the oscillation direction."
    },
    {
      "index": 52,
      "blockIndex": 22,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The source also states that Fresnel's formulas for the phase and amplitude of reflected and refracted waves remain important.\nThis passage describes what those formulas concern without giving the formulas themselves, so none are added here."
    }
  ],
